<meta type=article>
  <title | Enscribe Authoring Guide>
  <author | The Enscribe Project>
</meta>

This is the reference you keep open while writing. It answers "how do I do X" —
the exact syntax, the available arguments, the edge cases — for every element
Enscribe offers. It assumes you have done the
<a href="quickstart.html" | Quickstart> (or have equivalent familiarity) and
understand Enscribe's three-layer idea: most elements can be written three ways —
a **canonical** named tag, a **sigil** shorthand, and a **markdown** idiom — and
all of them compile to the same Layer 1 HTML. Where three forms exist, this guide
shows all three; where an element has only one form, it says so.

Each section is self-contained: you can read "Inline elements" without reading
anything before it. Jump to what you need. This guide is itself an Enscribe
document, so wherever it is useful it does not just describe an element — it
*uses* it, and the rendered result sits right below the source. This installment
covers chapters 1–9; the remaining chapters are listed at the end.

<# #sec:structure | Chapter 1. Document structure #>

Every Enscribe document opens with a `<meta>` block declaring its type, and may
carry a `<config>` block of document-level settings. These are *apparatus* — they
describe the document rather than belong to its body — so by convention `<meta>`
sits at the very top and `<config>` (with `<data>`) sits at the end.

**The `<meta>` block.** `<meta>` carries the document's identity and front
matter. Its `type` attribute picks the document shape — `article` (flat) or
`book` (chaptered):

```
<meta type=article>
  <title | A Short Account of Calibration>
  <author | Dana Okonkwo>
  <date | 2026-05-31>
</meta>
```

The child tags — `<title>`, `<author>`, `<date>` — are written in **pipe form**
(`<tag | content>`). `<meta>` and its children are canonical-only: there is no
sigil or markdown shorthand for them, because document metadata is explicit by
nature. Rendering a `<meta>` block mid-page would be disruptive, so here is its
Layer 1 output instead — the block becomes the document's front matter, and
`<title>` becomes `<article-title>` (or `<book-title>` in a book):

```
<article>
  <article-front>
    <meta data-document-type="article">
      <article-title>A Short Account of Calibration</article-title>
      <author>Dana Okonkwo</author>
      <date>2026-05-31</date>
    </meta>
  </article-front>
  …
```

A document may carry more front matter than this — abstracts, affiliations — but
title, author, and date are the core three. This very page opens with a `<meta>`
block carrying the title and author you saw at the top.

**Article versus book.** `type=article` produces a flat document: body content,
then back matter. `type=book` expects chapters and parts and produces a chaptered
document. The choice is structural, not cosmetic — it changes how counters reset
(figures and equations number within each chapter in a book), where footnotes
collect, and where the bibliography lands. Book structure has its own chapter
(forthcoming); for a journal-style article, `type=article` is what you want.

**The `<config>` block.** `<config>` sets document-level options as keyword
attributes. It is apparatus, so it goes at the end of the document:

```
<config citation-style="apa" number-figures=false>
</config>
```

The settings that are live today control citations, numbering, and scoping:
`citation-style` (the CSL style name passed to the citation engine);
`number-equations`, `number-figures`, `number-tables`, `number-theorems`,
`number-definitions`, `number-examples` (set any to `false` to suppress that
counter); `counter-reset-scope` and `note-scope` (how counters and footnotes
reset in books); and any `ref-prefix-*` key (to customize cross-reference
prefixes). Further keys — `theme`, `display-style`, and others — are reserved:
accepted but not yet consumed.

> **Gotcha — `<config>` is not the rendering API.** Options like
> `embedResources` and `dslMode` are passed to the renderer when you process a
> document (covered in the Rendering chapter, forthcoming); they are *not*
> `<config>` keys. A kwarg `<config>` does not recognize is dropped with a console
> warning rather than rendered, so a misplaced `embedResources=false` inside
> `<config>` silently does nothing.

*Related:* <a href="#sec:sections" | Sections and headings> (Chapter 2);
book structure and the `<data>`/`<library>` block are covered in later chapters.

<# #sec:sections | Chapter 2. Sections and headings #>

Section headings divide the body. Enscribe has three heading levels, each
available in all three forms, and all forms compile to the same Layer 1
`<section>` (or `<sub-section>`, `<sub-sub-section>`) wrapping a matching title
element.

**The three forms, side by side.** These three lines produce identical output —
a `<section>` titled "Methods":

```
<# Methods #>           sigil form: the fewest keystrokes
<section | Methods>     canonical form: explicit; what the others become
# Methods               markdown form: a single leading hash
```

All three reduce to the same Layer 1:

```
<section id="sec:methods">
  <section-title>Methods</section-title>
</section>
```

You are looking at the rendered result right now: every "Chapter N" heading in
this guide is a `<# … #>` section, and the bold heading text is a
`<section-title>`. The level is the number of hashes, and it is the same across
the sigil and markdown forms:

```
<# Title #>     # Title       →  <section>           (level 1)
<## Title ##>   ## Title       →  <sub-section>       (level 2)
<### Title ###> ### Title      →  <sub-sub-section>   (level 3)
```

The sigil form (`<# … #>`) is the recommended default for most authoring: it is
the lightest to type and still carries ids and attributes. The canonical
`<section>` is the explicit form — exactly what you would read if you converted
the finished HTML back to shorthand. The markdown `#` is there so muscle memory
works on day one; it lifts to the canonical section automatically, but it is a
*lossy* convenience — it has nowhere to put an id, so Layer 1 never round-trips
back to it.

**Section ids.** Give a section an id when something will cross-reference it, by
placing `#name` before the pipe:

```
<# #sec:methods | Methods #>
```

This renders `<section id="sec:methods">`, which a `<ref>` or an
`<a href="#sec:methods" | …>` link can target. Ids on sections use the same `#id`
syntax used everywhere else in Enscribe.

**Deeper headings.** The ladder stops at three section levels on purpose — three
is what a journal article needs. A fourth-level markdown heading and beyond do not
create sections; they pass through as plain HTML heading elements:

```
#### Subhead       →  <h4>Subhead</h4>
##### Subhead       →  <h5>Subhead</h5>
```

These are ordinary headings with no sectioning semantics, no id slot, and no role
in numbering. If a document genuinely needs to nest deeper than three levels, it
usually wants to be a *book* — chapters and parts, a different top-level shape —
rather than a fourth heading level. In books, sections number automatically within
each chapter; the details are in the cross-references and book-structure chapters.

> **Gotcha — hash count is the level, not the importance.** In ordinary Markdown
> `#` is the document's top title. In Enscribe the title comes from `<meta>`, so
> `#` is the first *body* section, `##` a sub-section, `###` a sub-sub-section.
> Match the sigil hash count to the markdown hash count: `<# … #>` and `# …` are
> both level 1.

*Related:* <a href="#sec:structure" | Document structure> (Chapter 1);
<a href="#sec:xref" | Cross-references> (Chapter 9) and book numbering.

<# #sec:inline | Chapter 3. Inline elements #>

Inline elements format a run of text inside a paragraph. The formatting tags each
support two Enscribe spellings — **same-line long form** (`<b>bold</b>`) and
**pipe form** (`<b | bold>`) — and both produce the same output, so use whichever
reads better. Bold, italic, and strikethrough additionally have markdown idioms.

**Bold and italic.** These have familiar markdown forms most authors will reach
for; the tags are for when you want the element explicit:

```
**bold**   <b>bold</b>    <b | bold>      →  <b>bold</b>
*italic*   <i>italic</i>  <i | italic>    →  <i>italic</i>
```

For example: **bold** and *italic* render as you would expect, and markdown
`_underscores_` produce italic identically to `*asterisks*`.

**Strikethrough, underline, quotation.** Strikethrough has a markdown form
(`~~text~~`); underline and inline quotation do not, so use the tag:

```
~~struck~~   <s>struck</s>   <s | struck>     →  <s>struck</s>
             <u>under</u>    <u | under>      →  <u>under</u>
             <q>quoted</q>   <q | quoted>     →  <q>quoted</q>
```

Rendered, those are <s>struck through</s>, <u>underlined</u>, and
<q>a short quotation</q>, set inline. `<q>` is HTML's inline-quotation element (a
browser renders it with quotation marks); reserve it for short in-line quotes, and
use a blockquote (Chapter 4) for anything that stands on its own.

**Inline code.** Backticks are the markdown form, and a sigil form exists for
symmetry with the other sigils:

```
`code()`        <` code() `>      →  <code>code()</code>
```

Backticks are almost always what you want — rendered, `code()` sets in a monospace
run. Content inside inline code is verbatim: no idioms or tags are interpreted
within it.

**Inline math.** Mathematics inside a run of text uses LaTeX dollar signs, handed
to KaTeX <cite @katex>; the sigil form is the canonical spelling:

```
$\varphi = (1+\sqrt5)/2$        <$ \varphi = (1+\sqrt5)/2 $>
```

Rendered inline, that is the golden ratio $\varphi = (1+\sqrt5)/2$, set right in
the run of text. Both forms produce the same selectable, scalable HTML math;
everything between the delimiters is LaTeX, passed through verbatim.

**Superscript and subscript.** Brace-delimited sigils set super- and subscripts:

```
x^{2}     →  x<sup>2</sup>
H_{2}O    →  H<sub>2</sub>O
```

Rendered, those are x^{2} and H_{2}O. The braces are required — `x^2` without them
is literal text — and they compose, so `x^{y_{1}}` nests a subscript inside a
superscript.

**Links.** Enscribe uses the `<a>` tag, not the markdown `[text](url)` form (which
Enscribe does not support). The URL is the first positional argument, or the
`href` keyword; the text is the body:

```
<a https://example.com | Example>
<a href="https://example.com" | Example>
<a href="https://example.com">Example</a>   →  <a href="https://example.com">Example</a>
```

Rendered, that is a link to <a href="https://example.com" | example.com>.

> **Gotcha — when to use `href=`.** The bare positional URL is fine for ordinary
> URLs, but a URL that is *just* a fragment (`#sec:methods`) collides with the
> `#id` syntax, and a URL containing spaces or a `>` will end the tag early. In
> those cases use the quoted `href` keyword form:
> `<a href="#sec:methods" | jump to Methods>`.

*Related:* <a href="#sec:block" | Block elements> (Chapter 4) for display math and
code blocks; <a href="#sec:xref" | Cross-references> (Chapter 9) link by name
rather than URL.

<# #sec:block | Chapter 4. Block elements #>

Block elements stand on their own between paragraphs.

**Paragraphs** are implicit, exactly as in Markdown <cite @commonmark>: write
prose, and separate paragraphs with a blank line. There is no paragraph tag to
type in ordinary writing.

**Blockquotes.** The markdown `>` works; the canonical `<blockquote>` works
same-line or multi-line:

```
> A quoted line.

<blockquote>A quoted line.</blockquote>

<blockquote | A quoted line.>
```

Rendered, the markdown form produces:

> A blockquote, set off from the body. Its content is ordinary Enscribe, so
> formatting like *italic* and inline math $e^{i\pi}+1=0$ are interpreted normally.

The markdown form wraps its content in a paragraph (`<blockquote><p>…</p>`); the
tag forms with inline content do not.

**Code blocks.** A fenced block is the standard form; add a language name after
the opening fence for syntax highlighting. The triple-backtick *sigil* is the
canonical equivalent. Both appear below — the outer fence here is four backticks
so the three-backtick examples inside it display literally:

````
```python
def f(x):
    return x + 1
```

<``` python | def f(x): return x + 1 ```>
````

Both render `<pre><code class="language-python">…</code></pre>`. Code-block content
is verbatim — no idioms or tags are interpreted inside it — and the block can take
an id (an `#code:f` token after the language) for cross-referencing. There is no
`<code-block>` named tag to author directly; the fenced block and the
triple-backtick sigil are the two forms.

**Display math.** Mathematics that deserves its own centered, numbered line is
*display* math. Write it as a `$$` fence with the delimiters on their own lines,
or with the canonical doubled-dollar sigil:

```
$$
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} e^{-x^2}\,dx = \sqrt{\pi}
$$

<$$ #eqn:gauss | \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} e^{-x^2}\,dx = \sqrt{\pi} $$>
```

Rendered, with an id so <a href="#sec:xref" | Chapter 9> can point back at it, the
Gaussian integral sets on its own numbered line:

<$$ #eqn:gauss | \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} e^{-x^2}\,dx = \sqrt{\pi} $$>

Display math is numbered automatically; give it an id (`#eqn:name`) when prose
will refer back to it.

> **Gotcha — `$$x$$` on one line is *inline*, not display.** The `$$` fence only
> produces display math when the delimiters are on their own lines. A single-line
> `$$x = y$$` renders as inline math. If you want display math without a multi-line
> fence, use the sigil `<$$ | x = y $$>`, which is always display regardless of
> line placement.

**Math environments.** LaTeX-style multi-line math environments are available as
tags: `<align>`, `<eqnarray>`, `<cases>`, and `<matrix>`. They carry verbatim
LaTeX and render through KaTeX as display blocks:

```
<align>
a &= b + c \\
d &= e + f
</align>
```

Use the long form (opener and closer on their own lines) for multi-row content;
the pipe form works for short cases. The aligned block is numbered like other
display math.

*Related:* <a href="#sec:inline" | Inline elements> (Chapter 3) for inline math
and inline code; <a href="#sec:fig" | Figures> (Chapter 5).

<# #sec:fig | Chapter 5. Figures and images #>

A figure is a numbered, captioned block for an image or other graphic. The element
is `<fig>`; `<figure>` is an authoring alias that rewrites to it, so both names
work and produce the same output. Figures are *frameable* — like tables and the
math environments, they get an automatic number, an optional caption, and an
optional border from one shared mechanism.

**Forms.** Figures are canonical-only — there is no sigil or markdown shorthand.
Markdown's `![alt](url)` inline-image form is deliberately *not* supported: a
figure in scholarly writing is a numbered, cross-referenceable block, not an inline
image, so Enscribe models it as a structured element. The `src` kwarg names the
image; the pipe content is the caption:

```
<fig src="diagram.png" | The pipeline in one breath.>
<figure src="diagram.png" | The pipeline in one breath.>   (alias — identical)
<fig #fig:flow src="diagram.png" | The pipeline in one breath.>   (with an id)
<fig src="diagram.png" />   (self-closing — an image with no caption)
```

**Arguments.** `src` is the image URL or path. The caption is the pipe content
(and also becomes the image's `alt` text unless you set `alt=` explicitly). `#id`
(conventionally `#fig:name`) makes the figure a cross-reference target. `width`
sets a suggested display width (a CSS length like `width="60%"` or `width="400px"`),
emitted as `data-width` for the theme to honor; figures do not take a separate
height — the image's aspect ratio is preserved. `+border` opts into a framed
border.

Here is a rendered figure, with an id Chapter 9 refers to:

<fig #fig:example src="https://picsum.photos/seed/enscribe-guide/640/360" | A placeholder image standing in for a real diagram. The caption is numbered automatically — you never type "Figure 1".>

The label ("Figure 1.") and the number are added for you; move the figure and the
number follows.

> **Gotcha — captionless figures still number.** A self-closing
> `<fig src="…" />` with no caption is still a numbered figure and still
> participates in the figure sequence. If you want an image that is *not* a
> numbered figure, that is a plain inline image — a different need Enscribe does
> not fold into `<fig>`.

For vector graphics, the simplest path today is to reference an external SVG file
as the image: `<fig src="diagram.svg" | …>` renders it like any other image.
Enscribe also has a dedicated `<svg>` element that shares this same frameable
surface; embedding inline SVG source through it is still being finished, so prefer
the `<fig src="…svg">` route for now.

*Related:* <a href="#sec:tab" | Tables> (Chapter 6) — the other common frameable;
<a href="#sec:xref" | Cross-references> (Chapter 9) to point at a figure by id.

<# #sec:tab | Chapter 6. Tables #>

Academic tables are mostly *data*, and writing data as HTML rows is miserable.
Enscribe lets you write the rows as CSV or TSV and supplies the table scaffolding —
the element, the header row, the cells — for you. Tables are frameable, so they
take a caption and an automatic number like figures.

**Forms.** The data formats — CSV and TSV — are canonical Enscribe; the markdown
**pipe table** is the markdown form. All three produce the same Layer 1 `<table>`.
`csv` and `tsv` are *positional qualifiers* on `<table>` (a bare token, no
`key=`), naming how to read the pipe content:

```
<table csv | Name,Score
Ada,10
Bo,7>

<table tsv | Name<TAB>Score
Ada<TAB>10>

| Name | Score |     ← markdown pipe table
| ---- | ----- |
| Ada  | 10    |
```

The first row is the header (rendered as `<th>` cells); each line after it is a
data row.

**Arguments.** `caption=` sets the caption text. `#id` (conventionally
`#tab:name`) makes the table a cross-reference target. Here is a rendered CSV
table, with an id and a caption, that Chapter 9 refers to:

<table #tab:comparison csv caption="The three section forms compared — the same data this guide describes in prose, written as four lines of CSV." |
Form,Keystrokes,Carries an id
Sigil,fewest,yes
Canonical,most,yes
Markdown,few,no
>

The "Table 1." label and number are supplied for you, exactly as with figures.

> **Gotcha — commas and tabs are the delimiters.** In a `csv` table a literal
> comma in a cell needs quoting (standard CSV rules); if your data is full of
> commas, `tsv` (tab-separated) is often cleaner. The markdown pipe form is handy
> for a small hand-written table but has no place to put an id or a caption kwarg,
> so reach for `csv`/`tsv` when the table needs to be referenced or captioned.

*Related:* <a href="#sec:fig" | Figures> (Chapter 5) — the sibling frameable;
<a href="#sec:xref" | Cross-references> (Chapter 9).

<# #sec:cite | Chapter 7. Citations and bibliography #>

Citations are keys into a bibliography you supply. You hold the *keys* and the
chosen *style*; the formatting, ordering, and disambiguation are delegated to a
citation engine and the CSL styles the rest of scholarly publishing uses.

**Forms.** Citations are canonical-only. Three pieces work together:

```
<data>
<library |
@article{smith2020, author = {Smith, A.}, title = {On Calibration}, year = {2020}}
>
</data>

… in the prose:  as Smith showed <cite @smith2020> …

<bibliography>
</bibliography>
```

The `<library>` block holds BibTeX entries and lives inside the `<data>` apparatus
block at the foot of the document. `<cite @key>` places an in-text citation.
`<bibliography></bibliography>` marks where the formatted reference list renders
(end of an article; per the book back matter in a book). An external library is
written `<library src="refs.bib">` instead of inline content, for when the
bibliography lives in a separate file.

**Arguments and multiple keys.** One `<cite>` can carry several comma-separated
keys, which render as a single grouped citation:

```
<cite @smith2020>                 single
<cite @smith2020, @jones2019>     grouped
```

This guide already cites as it goes — the KaTeX reference in
<a href="#sec:inline" | Chapter 3> and the CommonMark reference in
<a href="#sec:block" | Chapter 4> are `<cite @katex>` and `<cite @commonmark>`,
both resolving against the `<library>` in the `<data>` block at the foot of this
page. Grouped, those two are <cite @katex, @commonmark>, and the formatted list of
everything cited appears under "References" at the very bottom.

> **Gotcha — a `<cite>` needs its key in the library.** A `<cite @missing>` whose
> key is not in any `<library>` renders a visible "missing citation" marker rather
> than silently vanishing, so unresolved keys are easy to catch. Keep the `<data>`
> block's BibTeX keys and your `<cite>` keys in sync.

*Related:* <a href="#sec:foot" | Footnotes> (Chapter 8) — the other apparatus that
collects at the document's foot; <a href="#sec:xref" | Cross-references>
(Chapter 9).

<# #sec:foot | Chapter 8. Footnotes #>

A footnote attaches a numbered aside to a point in the text. The element is
`<note>`, written in pipe form immediately after the word it attaches to, with no
space.

**Forms.** Footnotes are canonical-only:

```
… the procedure is standard.<note | But see the appendix for the edge case.>
```

The marker is numbered automatically; the note's content collects into a list at
the end of the article (or per-chapter in a book — see `note-scope` in
<a href="#sec:structure" | Chapter 1>). This sentence carries a
footnote<note | Here is the footnote body. On the docs site the marker is a hover
target — hover or tap it to read this without losing your place — and the same
text also collects in the list at the foot of the page.> you can try right now.

On surfaces that support it the marker shows a **hover preview**; in print, or
where hover is unavailable, the note falls back to the numbered list at the foot of
the document. Same source, different surfaces.

> **Gotcha — attach the note with no space.** Write the `<note | …>` flush against
> the word it annotates (`standard.<note | …>`, not `standard. <note | …>`); a
> space before the marker reads as a space between the word and its footnote
> number.

*Related:* <a href="#sec:cite" | Citations> (Chapter 7);
<a href="#sec:xref" | Cross-references> (Chapter 9).

<# #sec:xref | Chapter 9. Cross-references #>

A cross-reference points at a numbered element by its id and renders the right
word and number — "figure 1", "table 2" — computed after the whole document is
parsed, so it is always the number the target actually received. Move the target,
renumber nothing.

**Forms.** Cross-references are canonical-only. `<ref @id>` names a target by its
typed id; the prefix of the id selects the word:

```
<ref @fig:flow>    →  figure N
<ref @tab:data>    →  table N
<ref @eqn:gauss>   →  equation N
<ref @thm:main>    →  theorem N
```

The id-prefix conventions are `#fig:` (figures), `#tab:` (tables), `#eqn:`
(equations), `#thm:`/`#lem:`/`#cor:`/`#prop:` (the theorem family), `#def:`
(definitions), `#ex:` (examples), and `#sec:` (sections). The reference resolves
forward or backward freely.

Here it is at work. This guide gave its figure the id `#fig:example`, its table
`#tab:comparison`, and its display equation `#eqn:gauss`; referring to them —
<ref @fig:example>, <ref @tab:comparison>, and <ref @eqn:gauss> — resolves to the
numbers they received above, each a link you can follow.

> **Gotcha — sections are unnumbered in a flat article.** Figures, tables,
> equations, and theorems carry numbers, so a `<ref>` to them reads "figure 1",
> "table 1". Sections in an article are *not* numbered, so `<ref @sec:methods>`
> falls back to the id's tail ("methods") rather than "section 1". Numbered section
> references are a book feature: with chapter/section counter scoping a book
> numbers its sections and the references follow. To link to a section by custom
> text in any document, use a plain anchor — `<a href="#sec:methods" | the Methods
> section>`.

In a **book**, numbering is *scoped*: a figure in Chapter 3 numbers as "Figure 3.1"
and a cross-reference to it renders the chapter-prefixed number. Scoped numbering
and the book counters are covered in the book-structure chapter (forthcoming).

*Related:* <a href="#sec:fig" | Figures> (Chapter 5),
<a href="#sec:tab" | Tables> (Chapter 6), and
<a href="#sec:block" | Block elements> (Chapter 4) for the equation referenced
above.

<# What's next #>

This installment covers document structure, sections, inline and block elements,
figures, tables, citations, footnotes, and cross-references. Chapters on the
**theorem family**, **external DSLs** (Mermaid, ABC), **book structure**,
**arguments and the pipe**, and **rendering and output** are coming in subsequent
updates. For the architecture behind the canonical-form model, read the
<a href="design.html" | Design article>; for a guided tour of the whole feature
set in one page, see the <a href="quickstart.html" | Quickstart>.

